#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092260 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092116 :question/id #uuid "c986ab8f-a8a6-5721-bc8d-ba7ec19b95ea" :question/math-academy-id "e-727" :question/problem "Calculate $f'(x)$ if $f(x) = e^{5x +4}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $f(x)$ as the composite function\n\n$$\nf = e^u, \\qquad u=5x+4.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d f}{\\textrm d u} = e^u,\\qquad \\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} =5.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{u}\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =5{e}^{u} \\\\  & =5{e}^{5x+4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a83f86ab-a008-5487-b118-f1495a0fe4d6" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048749 :topic/math-academy-id 1114 :topic/title "Differentiating Exponential Functions"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051084 :topic/math-academy-id 1108 :topic/title "The Chain Rule for Differentiation"}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092512 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d6fe2c4c-f454-5aca-a824-b3a7d3b7906a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{e^{3x}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089244} {:answer/id #uuid "fd2c856f-0818-5870-a81c-c09468b81451" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "xe^{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089245} {:answer/id #uuid "33420c54-a5e9-5e50-8d8a-2b748db05ce4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3e^{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089246} {:answer/id #uuid "43e58a07-4775-5ba0-87bf-4177453776ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3xe^{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089247} {:answer/id #uuid "62505802-48e7-5721-a94d-ba4a709ec693" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089248}] :answer-field/id #uuid "c07e8ff0-97b2-55ed-aa61-6034627f1aa5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089243}] :question/id #uuid "2b5accbf-e263-55a8-aa47-3d5a01076c05" :question/math-academy-id "q-11816" :question/problem "Given that $f(x) = e^{3x}$, find $\\frac{df}{dx}$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092513 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a72f46cd-ae5e-5369-ac3a-906a711a6a72" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{e^{2x + 5}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089250} {:answer/id #uuid "b53db06b-92b9-5298-810d-18bcc71b09a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2xe^{2x + 5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089251} {:answer/id #uuid "181aab1e-8a13-55d1-af91-7902b69fdb42" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2e^{2x + 5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089252} {:answer/id #uuid "228c28d4-c54c-59fc-abc4-8bd4a76de28f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{2x + 5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089253} {:answer/id #uuid "4de1d19f-8a03-5aca-a314-5556b81aac11" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2x + 5)e^{2x + 5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089254}] :answer-field/id #uuid "aa7337e6-4d8a-5b6d-9fd4-37daba6415d4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089249}] :question/id #uuid "b27b93f8-5f19-566f-95e3-f0a0b21ec684" :question/math-academy-id "q-11817" :question/problem "Given that $f(x) = e^{2x + 5}$, find $\\frac{df}{dx}$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233098 :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f95fdc58-1eb7-5580-ab34-aae2969c5c74" :question/math-academy-id "q-107420" :question/problem "Calculate ${f}^{'}(x)$ if $f(x)={x}^{3}-{e}^{1-3x}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $y={e}^{1-3x}$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny={e}^{u},\\,u=1-3x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}={e}^{u},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-3.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{u}\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & =-3{e}^{u} \\\\  & =-3{e}^{1-3x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using the sum rule of differentiation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{3}-{e}^{1-3x}) \\\\  & =3{x}^{2}-(-3{e}^{1-3x}) \\\\  & =3({x}^{2}+{e}^{1-3x}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234086 :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f33e1827-8673-5c93-87ec-142118255194" :question/math-academy-id "q-48003" :question/problem "Given that $y=-3{e}^{5-x},$ find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny=-3{e}^{u},\\,u=5-x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=-3{e}^{u},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-1.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =-3{e}^{u}\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =3{e}^{u} \\\\  & =3{e}^{5-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314963 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c0cfd52e-1fc1-51eb-85e5-32d9d3dc6090" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3{e}^{-3x+5}+8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313486}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c0cfd52e-1fc1-51eb-85e5-32d9d3dc6090" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3{e}^{-3x+5}+8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313486} :answer-field/id #uuid "7fd73eaf-0df8-5718-b843-1cd77b6090a3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314351}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8593de99-5055-5455-bd33-e38ccd6c9851" :question/math-academy-id "q-340496" :question/problem "Calculate ${f}^{′}(x)$ if $f(x)=8x+{e}^{-3x+5}.$\n\n${f}^{′}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $y={e}^{-3x+5}$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny={e}^{u},\\,u=-3x+5.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}={e}^{u},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-3.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{u}\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & =-3{e}^{u} \\\\  & =-3{e}^{-3x+5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using the sum rule of differentiation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(8x+{e}^{-3x+5}) \\\\  & =8-3{e}^{-3x+5} \\\\  & =-3{e}^{-3x+5}+8.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314964 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "970e1936-ac2b-534a-8ed4-97d799d35e63" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313177}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "970e1936-ac2b-534a-8ed4-97d799d35e63" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313177} :answer-field/id #uuid "f6a75775-f14f-5a3f-b4e1-1d6d0f134ccc" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314681}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8caad42a-7778-5faa-ba43-6f75823507a3" :question/math-academy-id "q-340497" :question/problem "Find the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve $y=-4{e}^{2-x}$ at the point where $x=2-ln⁡(3).$\n\n$m=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny=-4{e}^{u},\\,u=2-x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=-4{e}^{u},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-1.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =-4{e}^{u}\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =4{e}^{u} \\\\  & =4{e}^{2-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve at $x=2-ln⁡(3)$, we have to evaluate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point as follows\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{x=2-ln⁡(3)} & =4{e}^{2-(2-ln⁡(3))} \\\\  & =4{e}^{ln⁡(3)} \\\\  & =4\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329850 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "016b59fa-7dd1-5430-8a0d-9d17b256b87e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8{e}^{4x-1}+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326950} {:answer/id #uuid "1a09b33b-87d5-53a9-afee-f8e02962d4c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{4x-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327160} {:answer/id #uuid "35f572ac-b945-560e-89f5-d45ed6858e1a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8{e}^{4x-1}+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327403} {:answer/id #uuid "bab77687-86e3-5b97-960a-d832566921c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8{e}^{4x-2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328563} {:answer/id #uuid "bfa829b5-29ee-5ba4-a1c8-72e5faa0588a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8{e}^{4x-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328611}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bfa829b5-29ee-5ba4-a1c8-72e5faa0588a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8{e}^{4x-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328611} :answer-field/id #uuid "a2f4dd66-f6d3-5f50-8ce9-b004c416733f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329462}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "dd0c521a-06b7-559f-8b78-1152ec8cbcd7" :question/math-academy-id "q-48007" :question/problem "Calculate ${f}^{′}(x)$ if $f(x)=-2{e}^{4x-1}+3.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $f(x)$ as the composite function\n\n$$\nf=-2{e}^{u}+3,\\,u=4x-1.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}=-2{e}^{u},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=4.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =-2{e}^{u}\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =-8{e}^{u} \\\\  & =-8{e}^{4x-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329901 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12a9f96f-82fe-597d-8f9c-ec74365b138f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=20ex\\cdot ln⁡(5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327093} {:answer/id #uuid "341d8922-42cf-54fa-9393-c8b46d49f2db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=5ex" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327386} {:answer/id #uuid "388c97a7-d581-5bec-9e47-92d37546c27d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=ex" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327428} {:answer/id #uuid "569457c0-25f6-5141-b92a-2099597b16ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=4ex" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327685} {:answer/id #uuid "ae9ebfb2-3fe0-510b-bcbc-62f5f335f2b0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=20ex" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328449}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ae9ebfb2-3fe0-510b-bcbc-62f5f335f2b0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=20ex" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328449} :answer-field/id #uuid "40293d79-60e8-50ac-8ded-e02ee37fe2cf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329311}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b6722d36-cd26-5e84-89df-067b2a93b540" :question/math-academy-id "q-48657" :question/problem "Find the equation of the tangent to the curve $y=5{e}^{4x}$ at the point where $x=\\frac{1}{4}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny=5{e}^{u},\\,u=4x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=5{e}^{u},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=4.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =5{e}^{u}\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =20{e}^{u} \\\\  & =20{e}^{4x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find the slope of the tangent to the curve at $x=\\frac{1}{4}$, we have to evaluate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{x=1/4} & ={(20{e}^{4x})|}_{x=1/4} \\\\  & =20{e}^{4(1/4)} \\\\  & =20e\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, the $y$-value of the point on the curve where $x={x}_{1}=\\frac{1}{4}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & ={(5{e}^{4x})|}_{x=1/4} \\\\  & =5{e}^{4(1/4)} \\\\  & =5e.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we find the equation of the tangent at that point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-5e & =20e(x-\\frac{1}{4}) \\\\ y-5e & =20ex-5e \\\\ y & =20ex\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329902 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3844e08c-6f9d-5cf6-a14e-cc030d9a01b0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{e}^{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327425} {:answer/id #uuid "a89f0a93-4737-553c-9bbe-0034bad64d50" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328394} {:answer/id #uuid "adef63ee-5bd4-5b53-b409-c9e308d93260" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328445} {:answer/id #uuid "c199d559-8e1d-58ab-8615-2d150595957a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{e}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328624} {:answer/id #uuid "d557a2be-477a-515d-91b2-d30108f2cab4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328801}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "adef63ee-5bd4-5b53-b409-c9e308d93260" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328445} :answer-field/id #uuid "d0d50368-ac7e-5bb9-86d5-22e01e5b4791" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329554}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fbf43982-c74c-540d-95a5-32f181ac4ea6" :question/math-academy-id "q-48658" :question/problem "Find the slope of the tangent to the curve $y=-3{e}^{5-x}$ at the point where $x=5-ln⁡(2).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny=-3{e}^{u},\\,u=5-x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=-3{e}^{u},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-1.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =-3{e}^{u}\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =3{e}^{u} \\\\  & =3{e}^{5-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the slope of the tangent to the curve at $x=5-ln⁡(2)$, we have to evaluate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point as follows\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{x=5-ln⁡(2)} & =3{e}^{5-(5-ln⁡(2))} \\\\  & =3{e}^{ln⁡(2)} \\\\  & =3\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating an Exponential Function with a Linear Exponent"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092261 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092118 :question/id #uuid "775dfc95-df31-5198-aeb2-4603b8386957" :question/math-academy-id "e-728" :question/problem "Given that $y= 3\\cdot 2^{1 - x},$ find $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny = 3\\cdot 2^u, \\qquad u=1-x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d u} = 3\\cdot 2^u\\ln(2),\\qquad \\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} =-1.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =3\\cdot {2}^{u}ln⁡(2)\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =-3\\cdot {2}^{u}ln⁡(2) \\\\  & =-3\\cdot {2}^{1-x}ln⁡(2).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2f4bfd24-dafd-57b3-940a-154a6e0acf3d" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092733 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8d9ee625-e773-554a-b6fd-1b00c950d2f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2 \\cdot 3^{2x}\\ln (3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090599} {:answer/id #uuid "a2e4f469-6799-5ce4-b565-a59f324b5ec8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4x3^{2x^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090600} {:answer/id #uuid "bdacec05-5c20-55b6-8105-e82bd5158002" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x3^{2x^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090601} {:answer/id #uuid "f7139b42-c4a4-5c78-ab9a-4c8bcf3ecb8f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4x3^{2x^{2}}\\ln (3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090602} {:answer/id #uuid "15755bfa-20fa-599b-b783-c67a495db7ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x3^{2x}\\ln (3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090603}] :answer-field/id #uuid "4acd11bc-cb2e-5a8e-a9ee-06ec6470c2d5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090598} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9c382a73-b479-5088-ba73-8174216969b9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4x{3}^{2{x}^{2}}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402979} {:answer/id #uuid "7bd5037d-c800-5b92-94d5-bf8378100f05" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x{3}^{2x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402980} {:answer/id #uuid "bc15acd8-2e05-54df-824b-49b27e8976d4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4x{3}^{2{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402981} {:answer/id #uuid "c86614c3-2ff7-5322-a6c3-9ce930da4214" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x{3}^{2{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402982} {:answer/id #uuid "098f7a16-97a6-5c7b-bd17-6d5da322792f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\cdot {3}^{2x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402983}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9c382a73-b479-5088-ba73-8174216969b9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4x{3}^{2{x}^{2}}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402979} :answer-field/id #uuid "08bb9372-bdf9-5f57-a4fb-f3aaaf1824d4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402978}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8711f34a-9cc5-5a70-9af6-219e0592f067" :question/math-academy-id "q-48565" :question/problem "If $f(x)={3}^{2{x}^{2}},$ find ${f}^{′}(x).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $f(x)$ as the composite function\n\n$$\nf={3}^{u},\\,u=2{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}={3}^{u}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=4x.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={3}^{u}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)\\cdot (4x) \\\\  & =4x\\cdot {3}^{u}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3) \\\\  & =4x\\cdot {3}^{2{x}^{2}}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092735 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aa029f20-9e8b-5be2-9aaf-e25b51c039d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(12x)3^{6x^{2} - 4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090611} {:answer/id #uuid "adc2037a-76a7-570a-a5b0-aad7e20b5c7b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(12x)3^{6x^{2} - 4}\\ln (3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090612} {:answer/id #uuid "cbe2a81f-2507-5d07-bb73-29c3696d08a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(12x - 4)3^{6x^{2} - 4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090613} {:answer/id #uuid "d255dee3-7ee2-5cfc-af90-221a41a1961e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(12x - 4)3^{6x^{2} - 4}\\ln (3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090614} {:answer/id #uuid "582b9da7-3b84-58f5-a115-eedbe6fbcb2e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(12x^{3})3^{12x}\\ln (3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090615}] :answer-field/id #uuid "d7d3b7cd-8fef-5ba2-8969-6b86fbad9f15" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090610}] :question/id #uuid "475f4a17-648a-5df1-a5af-24c4bc3218f3" :question/math-academy-id "q-48631" :question/problem "Given that $y = 3^{6x^{2} - 4}$, find $\\frac{dy}{dx}$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233099 :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5b5b538e-387a-5bc3-9ad6-828140780508" :question/math-academy-id "q-107458" :question/problem "If $f(x)={2}^{3-{x}^{3}},$ find ${f}^{'}(x).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $f(x)$ as the composite function\n\n$$\nf={2}^{u},\\,u=3-{x}^{3}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}={2}^{u}\\operatorname{ln}2,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-3{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={2}^{u}\\operatorname{ln}2\\cdot (-3{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =-3{x}^{2}\\cdot {2}^{u}\\operatorname{ln}2 \\\\  & =-3{x}^{2}\\cdot {2}^{3-{x}^{3}}\\operatorname{ln}2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234314 :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "cc156fa4-1ae1-5983-b7ca-740de211698e" :question/math-academy-id "q-71881" :question/problem "Given that $y=2\\cdot {5}^{x+{x}^{3}},$ find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny=2\\cdot {5}^{u},\\,u=x+{x}^{3}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=2\\cdot {5}^{u}\\operatorname{ln}5,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1+3{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =2\\cdot {5}^{u}\\operatorname{ln}5\\cdot (1+3{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =(1+3{x}^{2})\\cdot 2\\cdot {5}^{u}\\operatorname{ln}5 \\\\  & =(2+6{x}^{2}){5}^{x+{x}^{3}}\\operatorname{ln}5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244059 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "152cb325-9ed7-57e6-9c36-b431d68cdffb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2x\\cdot {4}^{{x}^{2}-1}ln⁡4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241362} {:answer/id #uuid "2e76f4b9-59bb-51c9-a297-586eb211cb2f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6x\\cdot {4}^{{x}^{2}-1}ln⁡4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241592} {:answer/id #uuid "33d1a60d-2b25-5917-b2e1-7176812399fd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6x\\cdot {4}^{{x}^{2}-1}ln⁡4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241637} {:answer/id #uuid "7ab59f64-3cac-5e93-b9c8-4dd4ce8cba35" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x\\cdot {4}^{{x}^{2}-1}ln⁡4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242259} {:answer/id #uuid "ec2442e6-3bcb-5d29-a607-74db0268944f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3x\\cdot {4}^{{x}^{2}-1}ln⁡4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243268}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "33d1a60d-2b25-5917-b2e1-7176812399fd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6x\\cdot {4}^{{x}^{2}-1}ln⁡4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241637} :answer-field/id #uuid "fdd15e38-3eee-5ed4-a0c4-348e7c224b10" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243881}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f637ee3d-e925-50b9-8fb1-4aa44482e5e0" :question/math-academy-id "q-107459" :question/problem "Given that $y=3\\cdot {4}^{{x}^{2}-1},$ find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny=3\\cdot {4}^{u},\\,u={x}^{2}-1.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=3\\cdot {4}^{u}ln⁡4,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2x.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =3\\cdot {4}^{u}ln⁡4\\cdot 2x \\\\  & =6x\\cdot {4}^{{x}^{2}-1}ln⁡4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244060 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "530546dd-8877-5ee4-a9a9-4e6037256c79" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\cdot {6}^{1-4x}ln⁡6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241920} {:answer/id #uuid "600e2c77-5502-5a78-b78c-316c9d3b0309" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\cdot {6}^{1-4x}ln⁡6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242042} {:answer/id #uuid "a399353b-dcb8-5ce5-8226-adfa95834745" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24\\cdot {6}^{1-4x}ln⁡6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242614} {:answer/id #uuid "a3a4777f-04c7-56f8-9d79-a2692ed255c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4\\cdot {6}^{1-4x}ln⁡6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242615} {:answer/id #uuid "f5f46ff0-e8a9-5bfe-82dc-8df71e3e5cb5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-24\\cdot {6}^{1-4x}ln⁡6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243356}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a3a4777f-04c7-56f8-9d79-a2692ed255c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4\\cdot {6}^{1-4x}ln⁡6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242615} :answer-field/id #uuid "cfbbf656-26a0-5730-8af0-76fb3508854c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243802}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a0d30f56-0d1c-5b77-92c9-4ebf415c4add" :question/math-academy-id "q-107461" :question/problem "If $f(x)={6}^{1-4x},$ find ${f}^{′}(x).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $f(x)$ as the composite function\n\n$$\nf={6}^{u},\\,u=1-4x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}={6}^{u}ln⁡6,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-4.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={6}^{u}ln⁡6\\cdot (-4) \\\\  & =-4\\cdot {6}^{u}ln⁡6 \\\\  & =-4\\cdot {6}^{1-4x}ln⁡6.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287951 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0bb72661-686b-55b7-acd2-2a3bd4990839" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9\\cdot {2}^{9x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284881} {:answer/id #uuid "0fa45466-3a87-5294-bb09-177ec594bc1e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284925} {:answer/id #uuid "7da55036-6972-52f3-847b-a31f95df7852" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{2}^{9x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285944} {:answer/id #uuid "968393cd-2595-5198-a30d-859a805cd56e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\cdot {2}^{9x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286156} {:answer/id #uuid "f0d61fb9-24f0-590c-861d-b0dc09f76a1f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\cdot {2}^{9x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286998}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "968393cd-2595-5198-a30d-859a805cd56e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\cdot {2}^{9x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286156} :answer-field/id #uuid "05333763-722c-5f4d-b56e-21e02dec7970" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287152}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "15807573-b299-5e98-85b6-db47d2fbe91c" :question/math-academy-id "q-1952" :question/problem "Given that $y={2}^{9x},$ find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny={2}^{u},\\,u=9x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}={2}^{u}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=9.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={2}^{u}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\cdot 9 \\\\  & =9\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\cdot {2}^{u} \\\\  & =9\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\cdot {2}^{9x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297340 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f743bf76-aa68-58e9-907f-2483aa71ceaf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}(5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296581}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f743bf76-aa68-58e9-907f-2483aa71ceaf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}(5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296581} :answer-field/id #uuid "47b334c0-adcd-5c15-b3b3-c3ba23b7d937" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296767}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "96fff31f-332c-5572-b655-bad437de2f44" :question/math-academy-id "q-312462" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)={5}^{{x}^{3}+1},$ find ${f}^{′}(x).$\n\n${f}^{′}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}$\\cdot \\,{5}^{{x}^{3}+1}$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $f(x)$ as the composite function\n\n$$\nf={5}^{u},\\,u={x}^{3}+1.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}={5}^{u}ln⁡(5),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=3{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={5}^{u}ln⁡(5)\\cdot (3{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =3{x}^{2}ln⁡(5)\\cdot {5}^{u} \\\\  & =3{x}^{2}ln⁡(5)\\cdot {5}^{{x}^{3}+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315038 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aa1743b3-cca5-5571-9ea3-78db4f0b711a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\operatorname{ln}(5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313309}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "aa1743b3-cca5-5571-9ea3-78db4f0b711a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\operatorname{ln}(5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313309} :answer-field/id #uuid "a3811faf-1cb9-5232-9104-02c745e33d3d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314452}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5d403927-9339-566e-9390-9903483d0c58" :question/math-academy-id "q-340969" :question/problem "Find the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve $y={5}^{x-{x}^{3}}$ at the point where $x=1.$\n\n$m=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny={5}^{u},\\,u=x-{x}^{3}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}={5}^{u}ln⁡(5),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1-3{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={5}^{u}ln⁡(5)\\cdot (1-3{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =(1-3{x}^{2}){5}^{x-{x}^{3}}ln⁡(5).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve at $x=1$, we have to evaluate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=1} & ={((1-3{x}^{2}){5}^{x-{x}^{3}}ln⁡(5))|}_{x=1} \\\\  & =(1-3(1)^{2}){5}^{1-(1)^{3}}ln⁡(5) \\\\  & =-2\\cdot {5}^{0}ln⁡(5) \\\\  & =-2ln⁡(5)\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329903 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2f178a09-59b9-5bd4-94f2-717a9d712eec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327354} {:answer/id #uuid "ce962925-563f-5203-9850-54b8e1527957" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7ln⁡(4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328749} {:answer/id #uuid "d0342368-9d6f-56bf-9428-8ca2563004ae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "28ln⁡(4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328765} {:answer/id #uuid "e44279a3-2028-5e38-9e46-7a6b15fa9ce2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328935} {:answer/id #uuid "e877c22b-b0a5-5c6f-80fc-dddf3ca707ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4ln⁡(4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328967}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d0342368-9d6f-56bf-9428-8ca2563004ae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "28ln⁡(4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328765} :answer-field/id #uuid "c745005c-c2dc-5006-b584-ff3b1f2fe9d2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329537}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ac2dcbe2-d530-570b-ae79-f45a337889ca" :question/math-academy-id "q-48659" :question/problem "Find the slope of the tangent to the curve $y={4}^{7x}$ at the point where $x=\\frac{1}{7}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny={4}^{u},\\,u=7x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}={4}^{u}ln⁡4,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=7.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={4}^{u}ln⁡4\\cdot 7 \\\\  & =7ln⁡4\\cdot {4}^{u} \\\\  & =7ln⁡4\\cdot {4}^{7x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the slope of the tangent to the curve at $x=\\frac{1}{7}$, we have to evaluate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{x=1/7} & =7ln⁡(4)\\cdot {4}^{7(1/7)} \\\\  & =7ln⁡(4)\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =28ln⁡(4)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point where $x=\\frac{1}{7}$ is $28ln⁡(4).$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a General Exponential Function Using the Chain Rule"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092263 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092119 :question/id #uuid "b15a88b5-e2c4-51c1-90d5-031c2a8c23d3" :question/math-academy-id "e-729" :question/problem "Given that $y=e^{-x^2},$ calculate $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}.$" :question/worked-solution "First we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny = e^u, \\qquad u=-x^2.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d u} = e^u,\\qquad \\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} =-2x.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{u}\\cdot (-2x) \\\\  & =-2x{e}^{u} \\\\  & =-2x{e}^{-{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "107eaf98-be8c-5fb1-ac4c-948b1e00df0a" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092618 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "265b541b-62a2-540d-bd79-5ef733742b3d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x{e}^{{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285123} {:answer/id #uuid "4a81ce5d-011d-51ee-bf9c-6333bf4dc66f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285478} {:answer/id #uuid "4b97230b-e5a8-5328-9497-45595399fb66" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{e}^{{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285492} {:answer/id #uuid "812af0ad-7d34-5c50-a85a-78aa2431dcbb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x{e}^{{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285975} {:answer/id #uuid "dedd5724-68fc-54e4-9b62-8ebbbd324624" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286818}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "812af0ad-7d34-5c50-a85a-78aa2431dcbb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x{e}^{{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285975} :answer-field/id #uuid "0cfdfe24-c411-57d2-aa42-0fbbe96ad9c7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287169}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0e52fa16-cf3e-5339-95e2-786fe7ef7e5d" :question/math-academy-id "q-1953" :question/problem "Find ${f}^{′}(x)$ for $f(x)={e}^{{x}^{2}}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $f(x)$ as the composite function\n\n$$\nf={e}^{u},\\,u={x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}={e}^{u},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2x.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{u}\\cdot (2x) \\\\  & =2x{e}^{u} \\\\  & =2x{e}^{{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092734 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8f53b1fe-17ee-5800-9a3f-82327590a8b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4x^{5}e^{x^{4} + 1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090605} {:answer/id #uuid "b344b273-973c-5cd4-9b78-f9f4b6c14df2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4x^{3} + e^{x^{4} + 1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090606} {:answer/id #uuid "16fb416e-e5bf-57d4-a129-dd88c945f90c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4x^{3}e^{x^{4} + 1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090607} {:answer/id #uuid "2ec445b1-9e9b-5639-9cba-48edb379db01" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4x^{4}e^{x^{4} + 1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090608} {:answer/id #uuid "63133f5d-41f8-5703-8b59-5583d2d92a0e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4x^{5} + e^{x^{4} + 1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090609}] :answer-field/id #uuid "566e0008-fcd5-5a48-a5f1-0d9f404686db" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090604}] :question/id #uuid "842ba2f6-4002-59f4-86c1-997627e51415" :question/math-academy-id "q-48628" :question/problem "Given that $y = e^{x^{4} + 1}$, find $\\frac{dy}{dx}$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233913 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b5dc7bc1-7cee-51ed-a77a-e3c95bd45d7d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-7{e}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232838}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b5dc7bc1-7cee-51ed-a77a-e3c95bd45d7d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-7{e}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232838} :answer-field/id #uuid "95689d09-12c1-5c25-ad3c-3364276260a0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232837}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "06ab12ca-1261-529b-b77e-4551b3fd2404" :question/math-academy-id "q-340948" :question/problem "Find the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve $y={e}^{-2{x}^{6}+5x}$ at the point where $x=1.$\n\n$m=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny={e}^{u},\\,u=-2{x}^{6}+5x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}={e}^{u},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-12{x}^{5}+5.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{u}\\cdot (-12{x}^{5}+5) \\\\  & =(-12{x}^{5}+5){e}^{u} \\\\  & =(-12{x}^{5}+5){e}^{-2{x}^{6}+5x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve at $x=1$, we have to evaluate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=1} & ={((-12{x}^{5}+5){e}^{-2{x}^{6}+5x})|}_{x=1} \\\\  & =(-12(1)^{5}+5){e}^{-2(1)^{6}+5(1)} \\\\  & =-7{e}^{3} \\\\  & =-7{e}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233914 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "eeafba33-9d2d-5c84-83d1-aba9c2f1d3a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3{e}^{-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232840}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eeafba33-9d2d-5c84-83d1-aba9c2f1d3a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3{e}^{-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232840} :answer-field/id #uuid "67d8ceb6-7fd7-562b-b684-7cdcaee0de6d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232839}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8c2fc6b2-b088-54ff-90dc-24056bf60933" :question/math-academy-id "q-340955" :question/problem "Find the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve $y={e}^{-3x}$ at the point where $x=\\frac{1}{3}.$\n\n$m=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny={e}^{u},\\,u=-3x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}={e}^{u},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-3.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{u}\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & =-3{e}^{u} \\\\  & =-3{e}^{-3x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve at $x=\\frac{1}{3}$, we have to evaluate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=1/3} & ={(-3{e}^{-3x})|}_{x=1/3} \\\\  & =-3{e}^{-3(1/3)} \\\\  & =-3{e}^{-1} \\\\  & =-3{e}^{-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235434 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "340d32ab-0292-55d6-ad2a-da14464f67e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\operatorname{cos}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234632}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "340d32ab-0292-55d6-ad2a-da14464f67e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\operatorname{cos}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234632} :answer-field/id #uuid "b6445949-abe5-548c-ab63-85eaeea6ef0f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235195}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b2e5f110-a6b6-523d-8a9c-2c0d4e144aec" :question/math-academy-id "q-167329" :question/problem "Given that $y=f(h(x)),$ calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n${e}^{-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $y$ is a composite function:\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{h},\\,h=-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}h}=\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{h},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}x}=-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}h}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{h}\\cdot (-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\  & =-2{e}^{-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,{e}^{-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235482 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8d81f09a-0feb-5148-a473-414d681185ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{cos}(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234761}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8d81f09a-0feb-5148-a473-414d681185ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{cos}(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234761} :answer-field/id #uuid "a9c18185-4f4a-5388-bff5-21e01260a961" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235182}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b4e678b8-dc07-54a9-88b9-306f52add57d" :question/math-academy-id "q-167783" :question/problem "Given that $y=f(h(x)),$ calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n\n| $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\,-$ | {{field-1}} |\n| --- | --- |\n| ${e}^{4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}$ |  |" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $y$ is a composite function:\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{h},\\,h=-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x).\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}h}=\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{h},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}x}=-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x).\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}h}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{h}\\cdot (-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)) \\\\  & =-2{e}^{-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)}{{e}^{4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244055 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ad776a9-f4a0-5e5c-a3f9-720c0f51d8db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5{x}^{4}{e}^{{x}^{5}+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241411} {:answer/id #uuid "2b4eb06f-e217-5ea4-b6a5-fd33dc42e528" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5{x}^{4}{e}^{{x}^{5}+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241563} {:answer/id #uuid "6f6b602b-df9a-5236-8240-b5610aba6892" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{5}{e}^{{x}^{5}+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242173} {:answer/id #uuid "830d7ceb-1451-565b-a032-3b85a06189f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}{x}^{2}{e}^{{x}^{5}+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242323} {:answer/id #uuid "ee1d283c-0aa8-54f8-8845-20de899f1a2a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{x}^{5}{e}^{{x}^{5}+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243290}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2b4eb06f-e217-5ea4-b6a5-fd33dc42e528" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5{x}^{4}{e}^{{x}^{5}+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241563} :answer-field/id #uuid "d9d8a733-77cf-5587-abae-2d5e6d3cb563" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243816}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6db51996-7191-5665-ba1d-2ca54d5c9e73" :question/math-academy-id "q-107437" :question/problem "Find ${f}^{′}(x)$ for $f(x)={e}^{{x}^{5}+1}.$" :question/worked-solution "First we express $f(x)$ as the composite function\n\n$$\nf={e}^{u},\\,u={x}^{5}+1.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}={e}^{u},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=5{x}^{4}.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{u}\\cdot (5{x}^{4}) \\\\  & =5{x}^{4}{e}^{u} \\\\  & =5{x}^{4}{e}^{{x}^{5}+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244056 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "100855dc-01a1-54eb-889b-5c5849671fed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{x}^{2}{e}^{{x}^{2}-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241318} {:answer/id #uuid "412016b5-7fcf-5318-88ca-74a5337fd439" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{e}^{{x}^{2}-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241764} {:answer/id #uuid "b3c2451f-e77c-598c-a29b-975bfafc93b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2x+2){e}^{{x}^{2}-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242750} {:answer/id #uuid "e6242e2c-3c54-5534-b3c3-9e6ba73a9681" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2x-2){e}^{{x}^{2}-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243224} {:answer/id #uuid "e95fa722-682a-5996-bbc5-42651cbf8584" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x{e}^{{x}^{2}-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243242}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e6242e2c-3c54-5534-b3c3-9e6ba73a9681" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2x-2){e}^{{x}^{2}-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243224} :answer-field/id #uuid "666980b0-ea06-5cc6-8aba-d89d9e6e8dd9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243640}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "30a7e2e3-900c-5659-9ff0-e1c7283ea786" :question/math-academy-id "q-107445" :question/problem "Given that $y={e}^{{x}^{2}-2x},$ find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny={e}^{u},\\,u={x}^{2}-2x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}={e}^{u},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2x-2.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{u}\\cdot (2x-2) \\\\  & =(2x-2){e}^{u} \\\\  & =(2x-2){e}^{{x}^{2}-2x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244057 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5e4e1491-e222-5802-886e-742231f78057" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242027} {:answer/id #uuid "7723f856-56f5-5fee-abc8-1ff5e841e23c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242235} {:answer/id #uuid "a1ea903b-13e1-5dd4-9c7a-d0effe5e2d14" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6{e}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242598} {:answer/id #uuid "a87df020-501d-5562-907f-215a3c896761" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{{e}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242659} {:answer/id #uuid "b5b77e66-762b-57c0-a0ff-fd2e1fa2a094" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{e}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242766}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5e4e1491-e222-5802-886e-742231f78057" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242027} :answer-field/id #uuid "902db5d9-f439-5129-b87b-ab1648bec205" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243706}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "61336957-36ed-5998-bb55-140d4de08baf" :question/math-academy-id "q-107449" :question/problem "Find the slope of the tangent to the curve $y={e}^{3{x}^{2}-3}$ at the point where $x=-1.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny={e}^{u},\\,u=3{x}^{2}-3.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}={e}^{u},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=6x.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{u}\\cdot 6x \\\\  & =6x\\cdot {e}^{u} \\\\  & =6x{e}^{3{x}^{2}-3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the slope of the tangent to the curve at $x=-1$, we have to evaluate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=-1} & ={6x{e}^{3{x}^{2}-3}|}_{x=-1} \\\\  & =6(-1){e}^{3(-1)^{2}-3} \\\\  & =-6\\cdot {e}^{0} \\\\  & =-6\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244058 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4f51954f-ef10-5b61-8e64-a3c5da15b5b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241888} {:answer/id #uuid "561e1348-b169-5b7b-9445-b0635e9a11e7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241956} {:answer/id #uuid "662b5321-520e-54cd-9509-03f4e29b7113" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242102} {:answer/id #uuid "d763b1fc-ea11-5f90-95d1-e07b6b76e1cb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243092} {:answer/id #uuid "ea081c7f-fadb-5983-817c-316ec3dc87d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243251}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ea081c7f-fadb-5983-817c-316ec3dc87d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243251} :answer-field/id #uuid "4a8beb4d-3f02-544b-a522-4d1a06ffa730" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243581}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f0b47caa-0fc0-55c4-b8be-d17c35ea6d3b" :question/math-academy-id "q-107452" :question/problem "Find the slope of the tangent to the curve $y={e}^{{x}^{3}-4x}$ at the point where $x=2.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny={e}^{u},\\,u={x}^{3}-4x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}={e}^{u},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=3{x}^{2}-4.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{u}\\cdot (3{x}^{2}-4) \\\\  & =(3{x}^{2}-4){e}^{{x}^{3}-4x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the slope of the tangent to the curve at $x=2$, we have to evaluate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=2} & ={(3{x}^{2}-4){e}^{{x}^{3}-4x}\\,|}_{x=2} \\\\  & =(3(2)^{2}-4){e}^{{2}^{3}-4(2)} \\\\  & =8\\cdot {e}^{0} \\\\  & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297339 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4bbc6124-c201-533d-8a89-7b77ca50d995" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20{x}^{4}{e}^{4{x}^{5}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295698}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4bbc6124-c201-533d-8a89-7b77ca50d995" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20{x}^{4}{e}^{4{x}^{5}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295698} :answer-field/id #uuid "93a5b787-ef54-5338-bc44-45f2d399376a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296938}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "487ee789-7ad7-529c-b59f-58c0bcaef3bf" :question/math-academy-id "q-312451" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)={e}^{4{x}^{5}},$ find ${f}^{′}(x).$\n\n${f}^{′}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $f(x)$ as the composite function\n\n$$\nf={e}^{u},\\,u=4{x}^{5}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}={e}^{u},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=20{x}^{4}.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{u}\\cdot (20{x}^{4}) \\\\  & =20{x}^{4}{e}^{u} \\\\  & =20{x}^{4}{e}^{4{x}^{5}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315037 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "804c89d6-ac48-5950-9eea-d3b372fd6ba9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12{e}^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313008}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "804c89d6-ac48-5950-9eea-d3b372fd6ba9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12{e}^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313008} :answer-field/id #uuid "54daf3d6-e597-5f82-adf6-49dc58401548" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314249}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "85e8601e-9018-5cbf-862d-85832e302cc6" :question/math-academy-id "q-340949" :question/problem "Find the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve $y={e}^{4{x}^{3}}$ at the point where $x=1.$\n\n$m=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny={e}^{u},\\,u=4{x}^{3}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}={e}^{u},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=12{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{u}\\cdot (12{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =12{x}^{2}{e}^{u} \\\\  & =12{x}^{2}{e}^{4{x}^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve at $x=1$, we have to evaluate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=1} & ={(12{x}^{2}{e}^{4{x}^{3}})|}_{x=1} \\\\  & =12(1)^{2}{e}^{4(1)^{3}} \\\\  & =12{e}^{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329898 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "33b1c38d-bd47-595a-85a4-aaa20457bc59" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6{x}^{2}{e}^{8-2{x}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327383} {:answer/id #uuid "87871190-a65e-5c1e-8282-1faf7bde4a6f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(8-6{x}^{2}){e}^{8-2{x}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328101} {:answer/id #uuid "b8909401-18d1-5019-a6f0-ca794f50ba31" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6{x}^{4}{e}^{8-2{x}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328540} {:answer/id #uuid "c387f8bc-a855-544f-9080-010895d03bd3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6{x}^{2}{e}^{8-2{x}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328643} {:answer/id #uuid "e092d66a-dbb8-5e12-ab82-47da6c4607df" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6{x}^{4}{e}^{8-2{x}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328899}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c387f8bc-a855-544f-9080-010895d03bd3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6{x}^{2}{e}^{8-2{x}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328643} :answer-field/id #uuid "025f7489-d455-5ba5-a862-9edaba9cd328" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329189}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "97112097-e97d-59a1-a70d-99c7a4daf89b" :question/math-academy-id "q-48629" :question/problem "Find ${f}^{′}(x)$ for $f(x)={e}^{8-2{x}^{3}}.$" :question/worked-solution "First we express $f(x)$ as the composite function\n\n$$\nf={e}^{u},\\,u=8-2{x}^{3}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}={e}^{u},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-6{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{u}\\cdot (-6{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =-6{x}^{2}{e}^{8-2{x}^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339005 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6de9ff01-0b1f-56cd-885f-31d83a6b64e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8x{e}^{{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337007} {:answer/id #uuid "767d6034-4f15-5263-ac09-9c05f7472792" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8{x}^{3}{e}^{{x}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337099} {:answer/id #uuid "863405a3-9a66-5ca8-8f7d-483681a818bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9{x}^{2}{e}^{8{x}^{4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337245} {:answer/id #uuid "bbec19e9-c6de-585e-af2b-e55511e50cc2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8{x}^{3}{e}^{2{x}^{4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337701} {:answer/id #uuid "dcd7048a-7f6e-500d-bd2e-6421b3f20cac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{x}^{2}{e}^{2{x}^{4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337952}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bbec19e9-c6de-585e-af2b-e55511e50cc2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8{x}^{3}{e}^{2{x}^{4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337701} :answer-field/id #uuid "19f83298-fbd1-5427-81bb-2dc30205845b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338323}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "44577a72-fe3b-5755-87e3-6deb6a748e27" :question/math-academy-id "q-71873" :question/problem "Given that $y={e}^{2{x}^{4}},$ find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny={e}^{u},\\,u=2{x}^{4}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}={e}^{u},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=8{x}^{3}.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{u}\\cdot (8{x}^{3}) \\\\  & =8{x}^{3}{e}^{u} \\\\  & =8{x}^{3}{e}^{2{x}^{4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating an Exponential Function with a Nonlinear Exponent"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092273 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092112 :question/id #uuid "f0c5bd75-dd47-5028-8eff-38c87e7411bf" :question/math-academy-id "e-7116" :question/problem "The diagram below shows a circle of radius $r$ centered at the origin. If the polar coordinates of the point $A$ are $\\left(3,\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\right)$ and $m \\angle AOB=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2},$ what are the Cartesian coordinates of $B?$\n\n![](images/a0/a0d06318ba26eee754bc3581f5a0f1c126eb0723f8aea2cac0fad075ee56e7f9.png)" :question/worked-solution "First, since $A$ has polar coordinates $\\left(3,\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\right),$ we must have $r=3.$ Also, since the points $A$ and $B$ both lie on a circle of radius $3,$ the $r$-coordinate of $B$ must also be $3.$\n\nNotice that one must traverse the circle counterclockwise to move from the point $A$ to point $B.$ Since the $\\theta$-coordinate of $A$ is $\\dfrac{\\pi}{4},$ the $\\theta$-coordinate of $B$ must be\n\n$$\n\\theta_B = \\dfrac{\\pi}{4}+\\dfrac{\\pi}{2} = \\dfrac{3\\pi}{4}.\n$$\n\nSo, the polar coordinates of $B$ are $\\left(3,\\dfrac{3\\pi}{4}\\right)$\n\nNow, we convert $B$ to Cartesian coordinates. To convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx = r\\cos\\theta, \\qquad y=r\\sin\\theta.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $B\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\tx &= r\\cos\\theta\\[5pt]\n\t\t &=3\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{3\\pi}{4} \\right)\\[5pt]\n\t\t &=3\\left(-\\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)\\[5pt]\n\t\t &=-\\dfrac{3\\sqrt 2}{2}\n\t}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $B\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\ty &= r\\sin\\theta\\[5pt]\n\t\t &= 3\\sin\\left(\\dfrac{3\\pi}{4} \\right)\\[5pt]\n\t\t &= 3\\cdot\\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\[5pt]\n\t\t &=\\dfrac{3\\sqrt 2}{2}\n\t}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $\\left(-\\dfrac{3\\sqrt 2}{2},\\dfrac{3\\sqrt 2}{2}\\right).$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "386a4f1a-4ee5-5bf7-aff2-e190c83f4529" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092705 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d121349d-31c9-5bac-922f-a3d7427c2e17" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-4\\sqrt{2}, 4\\sqrt{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090431} {:answer/id #uuid "d94ac2cb-77dc-5c8e-8b60-7ebe1cd327a1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-4, 2\\sqrt{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090432} {:answer/id #uuid "00cc892a-647a-5731-8038-c03a6486c06e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-4, 2\\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090433} {:answer/id #uuid "351d03cf-8046-5fde-9515-8c70b37ccacf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-4\\sqrt{2}, 4\\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090434} {:answer/id #uuid "6a204728-2a70-5b4e-a407-acd285e5b284" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-4, 4\\sqrt{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090435}] :answer-field/id #uuid "ab58c44e-78a4-588e-a3bb-863a1a4a410d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090430}] :question/id #uuid "1ce6ea90-167e-5e84-81ba-98cd4696c493" :question/math-academy-id "q-40920" :question/problem "![](images/d8/d8fd52f3c965f041ea3e9bbc4e8a9705c49ec95be105653d869a0443fa97fac9.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows a circle of radius $r$ centered at the origin. If the polar coordinates of the point $A$ are $(8, \\frac{π}{4})$ and $m∠AOB = \\frac{5π}{12}$, what are the Cartesian coordinates of $B$?"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092706 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ee1ace03-46fb-5027-9235-084445577994" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-3\\sqrt{3}, 2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090437} {:answer/id #uuid "255dd244-f72a-5bd1-aede-078a04b6d5d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-2\\sqrt{3}, 2\\sqrt{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090438} {:answer/id #uuid "3b187ede-27c7-5c96-98e5-d476e31f991b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-3\\sqrt{2}, 3\\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090439} {:answer/id #uuid "4ad01eed-2a28-57ef-8f83-88b52a9d0ee6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-3\\sqrt{3}, 3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090440} {:answer/id #uuid "6eb7380d-f51d-526c-a08f-ae184cdbb5bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-3, 2\\sqrt{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090441}] :answer-field/id #uuid "75eac4b1-2ea4-536a-9d02-dcb0cd0092c0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090436}] :question/id #uuid "8f2d9d99-cff8-53e7-a4e3-be764ae4d44f" :question/math-academy-id "q-40922" :question/problem "![](images/ee/eed8bcd0866e87fabdb6a87b8a0c4622c61871222c46e7b616927fc3ced4c129.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows a circle of radius $r$ centered at the origin. If the polar coordinates of the point $A$ are $(6, 210^{∘})$ and $m∠AOB = 75^{∘}$, what are the Cartesian coordinates of $B$?"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256423 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5da02127-9e0f-517d-b833-10a14ea966be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-3,3\\sqrt{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254500} {:answer/id #uuid "872e2a4f-200b-5af7-8500-67bf6abe3f22" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-3,3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254867} {:answer/id #uuid "aa36bbb1-b88c-5b72-bb8b-94823eb95074" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-3\\sqrt{2},\\sqrt{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255175} {:answer/id #uuid "d7f185a7-e803-56ef-9e38-6ce29b1a5f8f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-3\\sqrt{2},3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255586} {:answer/id #uuid "e8f56308-c9ed-5d20-99cf-9b1ba40c9435" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\sqrt{2},3\\sqrt{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255716}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5da02127-9e0f-517d-b833-10a14ea966be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-3,3\\sqrt{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254500} :answer-field/id #uuid "562137bc-c0ee-5964-b8f9-1d239f29ef6c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256088}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ec6a7f4f-9f9a-5a0c-bdce-ef6083c2204a" :question/math-academy-id "q-120593" :question/problem "![](images/48/48517c5d3fa340bed1cc7b5493f0099a72afa79d9e0690b79695afe018c8a05c.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows a circle of radius $r$ centered at the origin. If the polar coordinates of the point $A$ are $(6,\\frac{\\pi }{6})$ and $m∠AOB=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ what are the Cartesian coordinates of $B?$" :question/worked-solution "First, since $A$ has polar coordinates $(6,\\frac{\\pi }{6}),$ we must have $r=6.$ Also, since the points $A$ and $B$ both lie on a circle of radius $6,$ the $r$-coordinate of $B$ must also be $6.$ \n\nNotice that one must traverse the circle counterclockwise to move from the point $A$ to point $B.$ Since the $\\theta $-coordinate of $A$ is $\\frac{\\pi }{6},$ the $\\theta $-coordinate of $B$ must be\n\n$$\n{\\theta }_{B}=\\frac{\\pi }{6}+\\frac{\\pi }{2}=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.\n$$\n\nSo, the polar coordinates of $B$ are $(6,\\frac{2\\pi }{3})$ \n\nNow, we convert $B$ to Cartesian coordinates. To convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =6(-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =6(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(-3,3\\sqrt{3}).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256424 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4b0831bf-55aa-510a-918a-90cd99cdc701" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-2,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254315} {:answer/id #uuid "54a4837d-2402-5b85-885b-20b64294842f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2\\sqrt{3},2\\sqrt{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254408} {:answer/id #uuid "7962a6c2-849e-5bfd-90f7-16eb630fa284" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2\\sqrt{3},-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254745} {:answer/id #uuid "87c149ae-d2b4-5a7d-8b6f-ca93c28e9563" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-2\\sqrt{3},2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254874} {:answer/id #uuid "f870bd85-6f4b-5e70-909a-8712db2320fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-2,2\\sqrt{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255845}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f870bd85-6f4b-5e70-909a-8712db2320fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-2,2\\sqrt{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255845} :answer-field/id #uuid "da62c4ed-dbe1-5f2f-b50d-04b6985ed206" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256309}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "0eb03380-bb2e-5373-833c-3b08aac5da8c" :question/math-academy-id "q-120595" :question/problem "![](images/5f/5ff0219f3f76441dbffc770ce0b8f2481cca8c46b8aa832ab7893d472f3026ae.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows a circle of radius $r$ centered at the origin. If the polar coordinates of the point $A$ are $(4,\\frac{5\\pi }{6})$ and $m∠AOB=\\frac{\\pi }{6},$ what are the Cartesian coordinates of $B?$" :question/worked-solution "First, since $A$ has polar coordinates $(4,\\frac{5\\pi }{6}),$ we must have $r=4.$ Also, since the points $A$ and $B$ both lie on a circle of radius $4,$ the $r$-coordinate of $B$ must also be $4.$\n\nNotice that one must traverse the circle clockwise to move from the point $A$ to point $B.$ Since the $\\theta $-coordinate of $A$ is $\\frac{5\\pi }{6},$ the $\\theta $-coordinate of $B$ must be\n\n$$\n{\\theta }_{B}=\\frac{5\\pi }{6}-\\frac{\\pi }{6}=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.\n$$\n\nSo, the polar coordinates of $B$ are $(4,\\frac{2\\pi }{3})$ \n\nNow, we convert $B$ to Cartesian coordinates. To convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =4\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(-2,2\\sqrt{3}).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256425 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0484ad77-2f99-5c81-a6cb-a1158be44d88" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-2,-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253704} {:answer/id #uuid "15246ab6-3ecb-5633-9b4e-b73f8ed07f54" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-4\\sqrt{2},-4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253853} {:answer/id #uuid "31095a2e-0934-55af-abe5-a2f05014f85f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4,-4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254098} {:answer/id #uuid "3f307f37-58c4-56a4-a6dd-e9b99b686e71" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4,4\\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254213} {:answer/id #uuid "e1629e74-354a-56e5-bbe5-bfd524f9a56f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,-2\\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255670}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "31095a2e-0934-55af-abe5-a2f05014f85f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4,-4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254098} :answer-field/id #uuid "e62cfd60-2bdd-5ae1-8632-0391624de18c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256330}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "4cadc848-bed5-5538-a431-ac83515fffa4" :question/math-academy-id "q-120597" :question/problem "![](images/fd/fd38e4bed4f11f07f2c6901a74c7543655f5ff8a95b8b049e54ff4ff4dd409ee.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows a circle of radius $r$ centered at the origin. If the polar coordinates of the point $A$ are $(4\\sqrt{2},{150}^{∘})$ and $m∠AOB={165}^{∘},$ what are the Cartesian coordinates of $B?$" :question/worked-solution "First, since $A$ has polar coordinates $(4\\sqrt{2},{150}^{∘}),$ we must have $r=4\\sqrt{2}.$ Also, since the points $A$ and $B$ both lie on a circle of radius $4\\sqrt{2},$ the $r$-coordinate of $B$ must also be $4\\sqrt{2}.$  \n\nNotice that one must traverse the circle counterclockwise to move from the point $A$ to point $B.$ Since the $\\theta $-coordinate of $A$ is ${150}^{∘},$ the $\\theta $-coordinate of $B$ must be\n\n$$\n{\\theta }_{B}={150}^{∘}+{165}^{∘}={315}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nSo, the polar coordinates of $B$ are $(4\\sqrt{2},{315}^{∘})$\n\nNow, we convert $B$ to Cartesian coordinates. To convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({315}^{∘}) \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({315}^{∘}) \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(4,-4).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256426 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9724a5f6-c9f4-536a-a17b-5425407b9c7d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255002} {:answer/id #uuid "ea54590c-b0df-5003-9417-4c09af7236b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\sqrt{2},\\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255723} {:answer/id #uuid "eb87a981-771e-5056-8d37-a93f3b1e2bef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255730} {:answer/id #uuid "feab8e4b-ee4c-5116-8ef8-f1f48cf2bd2f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\sqrt{2},2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255909} {:answer/id #uuid "ff088a24-bea8-531d-8f92-d5dff5c8b72a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,\\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255913}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ea54590c-b0df-5003-9417-4c09af7236b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\sqrt{2},\\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255723} :answer-field/id #uuid "47c6223c-72f8-56bd-b93f-b161db175405" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256059}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "1f77f386-bd94-517d-b58d-73799834bcb9" :question/math-academy-id "q-120598" :question/problem "![](images/47/475bda1ee758774ec9b07ec12a0ed92e56c4b571fd39f6e1ecca439278106ea6.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows a circle of radius $r$ centered at the origin. If the polar coordinates of the point $A$ are $(2,{240}^{∘})$ and $m∠AOB={195}^{∘},$ what are the Cartesian coordinates of $B?$" :question/worked-solution "First, since $A$ has polar coordinates $(2,{240}^{∘}),$ we must have $r=2.$ Also, since the points $A$ and $B$ both lie on a circle of radius $2,$ the $r$-coordinate of $B$ must also be $2.$\n\nNotice that one must traverse the circle clockwise to move from the point $A$ to point $B.$ Since the $\\theta $-coordinate of $A$ is ${240}^{∘},$ the $\\theta $-coordinate of $B$ must be\n\n$$\n{\\theta }_{B}={240}^{∘}-{195}^{∘}={45}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nSo, the polar coordinates of $B$ are $(2,{45}^{∘})$\n\nNow, we convert $B$ to Cartesian coordinates. To convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡({45}^{∘}) \\\\  & =2(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡({45}^{∘}) \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(\\sqrt{2},\\sqrt{2}).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294889 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9601aa2e-abda-5507-ad25-c4cf0