\n$$\n\nSo, the polar coordinates of $B$ are $(4,{30}^{∘})$ \n\nNow, we convert $B$ to Cartesian coordinates. To convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡({30}^{∘}) \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{sin}⁡({30}^{∘}) \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(2\\sqrt{3},2).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294890 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8e3ea9aa-2309-5688-8b5e-dd148f86cfd5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293482}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8e3ea9aa-2309-5688-8b5e-dd148f86cfd5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293482} :answer-field/id #uuid "e142ea0a-9b5f-51f6-80c8-47978bf6ecdf" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294719} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c9b12f13-f42a-5b68-a20b-fb1c035b77fd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293714}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c9b12f13-f42a-5b68-a20b-fb1c035b77fd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293714} :answer-field/id #uuid "f2b0d2fd-d64a-5114-8b1b-0b8b6064a336" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294783}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "fb90e8f9-7bb2-5ebe-9317-d50976c6167d" :question/math-academy-id "q-277027" :question/problem "![](images/db/dbca0b9bd96eb68b7f874df74848bb887037a7de2ed7613bc848c40f78feff4f.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows a circle of radius $r$ centered at the origin. Determine the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ given that the polar coordinates of the point $A$ are $(10,{60}^{∘})$ and $m∠AOB={60}^{∘}.$\n\nThe Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$).$" :question/worked-solution "First, since $A$ has polar coordinates $(10,{60}^{∘}),$ we must have $r=10.$ Also, since the points $A$ and $B$ both lie on a circle of radius $10,$ the $r$-coordinate of $B$ must also be $10.$\n\nNotice that one must traverse the circle counter-clockwise to move from the point $A$ to point $B.$ Since the $\\theta $-coordinate of $A$ is ${60}^{∘},$ the $\\theta $-coordinate of $B$ must be\n\n$$\n{\\theta }_{B}={60}^{∘}+{60}^{∘}={120}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nSo, the polar coordinates of $B$ are $(10,{120}^{∘})$ \n\nNow, we convert $B$ to Cartesian coordinates. To convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =10\\operatorname{cos}⁡({120}^{∘}) \\\\  & =10\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =10\\operatorname{sin}⁡({120}^{∘}) \\\\  & =10\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\  & =5\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(-5,5\\sqrt{3}).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351267 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19aefefa-5028-5a51-9e4a-e7a5f85a0ecd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\sqrt{3},4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348661} {:answer/id #uuid "31c265f3-9b60-519e-8c42-a2074afe1991" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2\\sqrt{2},4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348888} {:answer/id #uuid "61e897c5-e855-5435-b9a3-3ce1fe3df228" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{9}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349343} {:answer/id #uuid "6bdd275f-98a8-5d3a-be25-406316b4b4e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{5}{2},\\frac{5\\sqrt{3}}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349442} {:answer/id #uuid "a8ea7143-6d50-55a0-8054-cb45eeb37658" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2\\sqrt{2},\\frac{9}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349952}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6bdd275f-98a8-5d3a-be25-406316b4b4e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{5}{2},\\frac{5\\sqrt{3}}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349442} :answer-field/id #uuid "7ce1ae29-1a77-542a-a894-431caec80306" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350924}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e4c53f6c-deac-537d-ad18-d2272f3b99d1" :question/math-academy-id "q-81221" :question/problem "![](images/6e/6e9d2238ca77532c3adda8d35f94061d108c3e15432ce48fdc33885ddf4f4e48.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows a circle of radius $r$ centered at the origin. If the polar coordinates of the point $A$ are $(5,\\frac{7\\pi }{6})$ and $m∠AOB=\\frac{5\\pi }{6},$ what are the Cartesian coordinates of $B?$" :question/worked-solution "First, since $A$ has polar coordinates $(5,\\frac{7\\pi }{6}),$ we must have $r=5.$ Also, since the points $A$ and $B$ both lie on a circle of radius $5,$ the $r$-coordinate of $B$ must also be $5.$\n\nNotice that one must traverse the circle clockwise to move from the point $A$ to point $B.$ Since the $\\theta $-coordinate of $A$ is $\\frac{7\\pi }{6},$ the $\\theta $-coordinate of $B$ must be\n\n$$\n{\\theta }_{B}=\\frac{7\\pi }{6}-\\frac{5\\pi }{6}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.\n$$\n\nSo, the polar coordinates of $B$ are $(5,\\frac{\\pi }{3})$\n\nNow, we convert $B$ to Cartesian coordinates. To convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(\\frac{5}{2},\\frac{5\\sqrt{3}}{2}).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351268 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a38cdd4-2914-5fbf-af5a-f896fca82487" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\frac{11}{2},-4\\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348534} {:answer/id #uuid "46388f94-0b9a-5819-926f-46042d3247f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-6,-4\\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349085} {:answer/id #uuid "46e780ae-e427-5db3-9e7b-87fd979da562" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-4,-5\\sqrt{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349091} {:answer/id #uuid "de7003e9-ed40-536d-a5eb-03ffaab61707" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-5,-5\\sqrt{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350392} {:answer/id #uuid "e8f16d9b-438b-5abd-801a-9c54b0392c42" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-5,-4\\sqrt{6})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350488}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "de7003e9-ed40-536d-a5eb-03ffaab61707" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-5,-5\\sqrt{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350392} :answer-field/id #uuid "07393c80-442e-58d3-986a-670d473a0e30" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350717}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ebfe6ff0-a2a2-5246-ac85-4546ec479cc9" :question/math-academy-id "q-81224" :question/problem "![](images/a6/a615f7a80b81f89fccd8c82bf65d65e3a4a65958ec8a6fbc746ae68749949a87.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows a circle of radius $r$ centered at the origin. If the polar coordinates of the point $A$ are $(10,\\frac{\\pi }{3})$ and $m∠AOB=\\pi ,$ what are the Cartesian coordinates of $B?$" :question/worked-solution "First, since $A$ has polar coordinates $(10,\\frac{\\pi }{3}),$ we must have $r=10.$ Also, since the points $A$ and $B$ both lie on a circle of radius $10,$ the $r$-coordinate of $B$ must also be $10.$\n\nNotice that one must traverse the circle counterclockwise to move from the point $A$ to point $B.$ Since the $\\theta $-coordinate of $A$ is $\\frac{\\pi }{3},$ the $\\theta $-coordinate of $B$ must be\n\n$$\n{\\theta }_{B}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}+\\pi =\\frac{4\\pi }{3}.\n$$\n\nSo, the polar coordinates of $B$ are $(10,\\frac{4\\pi }{3})$\n\nNow, we convert $B$ to Cartesian coordinates. To convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =10\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{4\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =10(-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =10\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{4\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =10(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\  & =-5\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(-5,-5\\sqrt{3}).$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Coordinates of a Point Using a Diagram"}]}
